    {
      "type": "done",
      "answer": "No, increasing the number of banks does not let one channel's return bandwidth grow without limit.\n\nAccording to the lecture, while banks provide independent storage arrays that allow work in one bank to overlap with preparation in another, the shared data bus still limits how much data the channel can return at once [S1]. The peak bandwidth of a channel is determined by the transfer rate and the bus width, not by the number of banks [S3]. Therefore, adding more banks improves latency hiding and concurrency but does not increase the maximum data throughput of the single shared data bus [S1][S3].\n\nSee page 36 [S1] and page 39 [S3].",
      "citations": [
        {
          "document_id": "ad66f2c8360da43ea0981bffa549956185949044062a0abe4689381ced423402",
          "chunk_id": "ad66f2c8360da43ea0981bffa549956185949044062a0abe4689381ced423402-p36-1",
          "page": 36,
          "text": "CPU Memory Hierarchy\n\nDRAM Behind Cache: Multi-Bank DRAM\n                 Addr and Cmd Bus\n\n       Memory\n        Controller\n\n\n                    Data Bus\n                   Channel\n\n\n\n\n                                    Multi-Banked DRAM Chip\n  Banks provide independent storage arrays. Work in one bank can overlap\n▪\n  preparation in another.\n\n▪ The shared data bus still limits how much data the channel can return at once.\n\n\nMemory Wall     CPU Memory    GPU Memory    SOTA Memory     Advanced AI Computing and Systems        36",
          "preview_url": "/api/documents/ad66f2c8360da43ea0981bffa549956185949044062a0abe4689381ced423402/pages/36/preview",
          "source": "S1"
        },
        {
          "document_id": "ad66f2c8360da43ea0981bffa549956185949044062a0abe4689381ced423402",
          "chunk_id": "ad66f2c8360da43ea0981bffa549956185949044062a0abe4689381ced423402-p39-1",
          "page": 39,
          "text": "CPU Memory Hierarchy\n\nDRAM Behind Cache: Channel\n\n  One memory-controller\n  port drives one channel\n  Command, address, and\n  clock signals select the\n  destination\n   All ranks share the data\n  bus; only the selected\n  rank may drive it\n\n\n\n\n                   Peak Bandwidth\n             = Transfer Rate x Bus Width / 8\n\n\nMemory Wall     CPU Memory    GPU Memory    SOTA Memory     Advanced AI Computing and Systems        39",
          "preview_url": "/api/documents/ad66f2c8360da43ea0981bffa549956185949044062a0abe4689381ced423402/pages/39/preview",
          "source": "S3"
        }
